<topic id="d89af9ef-56cd-4dd7-bc4e-db4f236357f4" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

   

  
    
        
            
            
                <para>
                    DemiCode Scheduler is the .NET 2.0 task scheduling component for your applications
                    and services. It is easily set up to raise events on given schedules that will call
                    into your application to run your code.
                </para>
                <para>
                    Typically you need to use schedules in your application to automate regular maintenance
                    tasks such as creating reports, backups, data import or export, data cleansing,
                    checking and sending out emails or other notifying about statuses and other tasks.
                </para>
                <para>
                    Built-in schedules and tasks can be set up to call your code a single time, at intervals or
                    on specific times every day or only named weekdays, specific dates and more. These
                    schedules can be combined to create new schedules, or you can easily create your
                    own classes to represent even more complex recurrence patterns.
                </para>
                <para>
                    The schedules can be initialized either automatically through the use of a configuration
                    file (.config file) or some other XML store, or programatically using any .NET compliant
                    programming language. You will find examples on how to do either one in the <link xlink:href="ac8cf6b9-a48f-4b86-b13d-2303a63255aa">                         samples section</link>.
                </para>
                <para>
                    See the <link xlink:href="5c63cb15-8536-4738-8fea-5062096071a2">DemiCode Scheduler documentation</link> for more information.
                </para>
            
            
                
                   
                
            
        
    

